2026/11/02 13:17:03 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 2: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
IMPORTANT GUIDELINES FOR IDENTIFYING HEADINGS:
1. OUTLINE HEADINGS are marked by Tibetan ordinal markers followed by "ནི།" or similar particles: དང་པོ་...ནི། ("the first is"), གཉིས་པ་...ནི། ("the second is"), གསུམ་པ་...ནི། ("the third is"), བཞི་པ་...ནི། ("the fourth is"), etc. These mark structural divisions in the commentary.
2. NOT ALL ORDINALS ARE HEADINGS: Ordinal markers like དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་ that appear in the middle of sentences or discussions are NOT outline headings. Only those that introduce new structural sections are headings.
3. RHETORICAL QUESTIONS (ཞེ་ན།, "if you ask...") are NOT outline headings. These are common in Tibetan commentarial style to introduce objections or questions, but they do not mark structural divisions.
4. DEPTH DETERMINATION: Headings with the same ordinal level (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་, བཞི་པ་, etc.) at the beginning of a section-introducing phrase are siblings. When a new set of ordinals (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་...) appears within a section already introduced by an ordinal heading, these new ordinals mark sub-sections and should be assigned depth+1. For example:
   - བཞི་པ་...ནི། (depth 1)
     - དང་པོ་...ནི། (depth 2, first sub-section of བཞི་པ་)
     - གཉིས་པ་...ནི། (depth 2, second sub-section of བཞི་པ་)
     - གསུམ་པ་...ནི། (depth 2, third sub-section of བཞི་པ་)
5. The leading ༈ symbol typically marks major section headings and should be included in the anchor if present.
6. Parenthetical clarifications like (བསྟན་པ་གནས་པའི་ཚད་) that restate the topic are part of the heading structure and should be included in the anchor.
7. Copy the anchor text EXACTLY as it appears in the passage, including all tshegs (་), shads (།), and special characters.
