2026/11/02 13:18:45 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 3: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
IMPORTANT GUIDELINES FOR IDENTIFYING HEADINGS:
1. OUTLINE HEADINGS are marked by Tibetan ordinal markers followed by "ནི།" or similar particles that INTRODUCE NEW STRUCTURAL SECTIONS: དང་པོ་...ནི། ("the first is"), གཉིས་པ་...ནི། ("the second is"), གསུམ་པ་...ནི། ("the third is"), བཞི་པ་...ནི། ("the fourth is"), etc. These mark structural divisions in the commentary.
2. NOT ALL ORDINALS ARE HEADINGS: Ordinal markers like དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་ that appear in the middle of sentences or discussions are NOT outline headings. Only those that introduce new structural sections are headings. Ordinals used to enumerate items within a discussion (like "the first cause is...", "the second type is...") are NOT headings.
3. RHETORICAL QUESTIONS (ཞེ་ན།, "if you ask...") are NOT outline headings. These are common in Tibetan commentarial style to introduce objections or questions, but they do not mark structural divisions.
4. DEPTH DETERMINATION: Headings are organized hierarchically based on their ordinal sequences. When a passage starts with བཞི་པ་...ནི། or any higher ordinal (fifth, sixth, etc.), followed by a sequence དང་པོ་...ནི།, གཉིས་པ་...ནི།, གསུམ་པ་...ནི།, the higher ordinal is depth 1 and the new sequence starting with དང་པོ་ represents depth 2 subsections. In general:
   - The first ordinal sequence encountered (or continuing from a previous context) establishes depth 1
   - When a NEW ordinal sequence begins (restarting from དང་པོ་), it represents subdivisions at depth+1 of the most recent higher-level heading
   - Example structure:
     བཞི་པ་...ནི། (depth 1)
       དང་པོ་...ནི། (depth 2, first sub-section of བཞི་པ་)
       གཉིས་པ་...ནི། (depth 2, second sub-section of བཞི་པ་)
     ལྔ་པ་...ནི། (depth 1, sibling to བཞི་པ་)
5. CRITICAL: Every passage exists within a broader structural context. The ordinal markers visible in the passage indicate which level of the outline you are viewing. If a passage begins with བརྒྱད་པ་ (eighth) and later contains ༈ གཉིས་པ་, this does NOT mean གཉིས་པ་ is a sibling at the same depth. The བརྒྱད་པ་ establishes the current depth level, and གཉིས་པ་ introduces a SUBSECTION within it (depth+1). When you see a RESTART of the ordinal sequence (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་... appearing after a higher ordinal), these are always subsections at increased depth.
6. The leading ༈ symbol typically marks major section headings and should be included in the anchor if present.
7. Parenthetical clarifications like (བསྟན་པ་གནས་པའི་ཚད་) that restate the topic are part of the heading structure and should be included in the anchor.
8. Copy the anchor text EXACTLY as it appears in the passage, including all tshegs (་), shads (།), special characters, and spacing variations (such as extra spaces ༷ that may appear in digitized texts).
9. STRUCTURAL CONTEXT: When a passage begins with a heading like བརྒྱད་པ་...ནི། followed by content and then ༈ གཉིས་པ་...ནི།, the བརྒྱད་པ་ is continuing an existing outline level from before the passage, and ༈ གཉིས་པ་ is NOT a sibling to བརྒྱད་པ་ but rather a subdivision within it or within the broader section that བརྒྱད་པ་ belongs to. Pay careful attention to whether ordinals form a continuous sequence (indicating same depth) or restart (indicating depth change).
