2026/11/02 13:19:32 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 4: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
Important patterns to recognize as outline headings:
1. Explicit ordinal markers followed by topic descriptions, such as:
   - དང་པོ་ ("first") + topic + ནི། or other particles
   - གཉིས་པ་ ("second") + topic + ནི། or ལ་གཉིས། ("has two parts") or ལ་གསུམ། ("has three parts")
   - གསུམ་པ་ ("third") + topic + ནི། or similar
   - བཞི་པ་ ("fourth"), ལྔ་པ་ ("fifth"), དྲུག་པ་ ("sixth"), བདུན་པ་ ("seventh"), etc.
2. Ordinal markers that introduce structural divisions create parent sections, while their sub-parts (marked by དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, etc. within the scope of a parent section) create child sections at the next depth level.
3. When a heading announces multiple sub-divisions (e.g., "ལ་གཉིས།" = "has two parts" or "ལ་གསུམ།" = "has three parts"), the subsequent དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་ markers introduce those sub-sections at one level deeper than the parent.
4. Parenthetical topic labels appearing after ordinal markers like དང་པོ་(རྣམ་ཤེས་གང་དང་མཚུངས་པར་ལྡན་) should be treated as headings, where the full heading text includes the ordinal marker and the parenthetical content.
5. The depth hierarchy follows the logical structure: a གསུམ་པ་ heading introducing a new major topic is at the same level as preceding གཉིས་པ་ or དང་པོ་ headings at that structural level, while དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་ markers appearing as subdivisions of a parent heading are one level deeper.
6. Every heading marked by ordinal numbers (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་, བཞི་པ་, etc.) should be extracted, regardless of whether it announces further subdivisions or is a terminal section.
7. Track the structural context carefully: when you encounter a heading that announces subdivisions (e.g., "གསུམ་པ་[topic]་ལ་གསུམ།"), this is itself a heading at the current depth, and its three parts will appear as དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་ at depth+1.
For the anchor field, copy exactly 40 characters starting from the beginning of the heading as it appears in the passage, preserving all Tibetan characters, particles, and punctuation marks.
