2026/11/02 13:20:27 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 5: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
Important: Outline headings are structural markers that introduce major or minor sections of the text. They are typically indicated by ordinal markers followed by a topic phrase and often end with phrases like "ལ་གཉིས།" (has two parts), "ལ་གསུམ།" (has three parts), "ལ་བཞི།" (has four parts), etc., or end with "འོ།" or "ནོ།" These headings announce the structure of what follows.
Do NOT treat lists of sub-topics (e.g., "ཟག་པ་དང༌། ཆུ་སྦྱོར་དང༌། ཉེར་ལེན།" or "སྤྱིའི་དོན་དང༌། གཞུང་གི་དོན་ནོ།") as standalone outline headings unless they are part of a sentence that functions as a structural announcement.
Ordinal markers include: དང་པོ་ (first), གཉིས་པ་ (second), གསུམ་པ་ (third), བཞི་པ་ (fourth), ལྔ་པ་ (fifth), དྲུག་པ་ (sixth), བདུན་པ་ (seventh), བརྒྱད་པ་ (eighth), etc.
The depth of a heading is determined by its hierarchical relationship to other headings. A heading introduced by "དང་པོ་" directly after a heading ending with "ལ་གཉིས།" or similar is a subsection (depth increases by 1). The first heading in the passage defines depth 1.
When determining depth, carefully track the hierarchical structure: if a heading announces subdivisions (e.g., "ལ་གཉིས།"), the next heading with "དང་པོ་" is a child of that announcement and should have depth incremented by 1. Continue tracking parent-child relationships throughout the passage.
