2026/11/02 13:22:20 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 7: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
Outline headings in Tibetan commentaries are marked by ordinal structural indicators such as དང་པོ (first), གཉིས་པ (second), གསུམ་པ (third), བཞི་པ (fourth), ལྔ་པ (fifth), etc., typically followed by topic indicators and structural phrases like ལ་གསུམ (has three parts), ལ་གཉིས (has two parts), ལ་བཞི (has four parts), or ནི། These markers introduce new sections or subsections.
The depth of a heading is determined by its hierarchical position in the outline structure, NOT by the ordinal number used. For example:
- A heading marked གཉིས་པ (second) can be depth 1 if it's a main section at the top level
- A heading marked དང་པོ (first) can be depth 2 if it's the first subsection under a depth-1 heading
- A heading marked གསུམ་པ (third) can be depth 2 if it's the third subsection under a depth-1 heading
To determine depth correctly:
1. Track the hierarchical parent-child relationships between sections based on the logical flow and nesting of the commentary
2. When a heading introduces subdivisions (e.g., "ལ་གསུམ" = has three parts), the subsequent དང་པོ, གཉིས་པ, གསུམ་པ sections are children of that heading
3. A section continues until a new section at the same or higher level begins
4. Sections marked with parenthetical clarifications like དང་པོ་(ཐོབ་མ་ཐོབ་) are typically subsections
Do NOT include every instance of དང་པོ་ནི or similar phrases as headings. Only include actual structural outline headings that divide the commentary into sections. Descriptive phrases that begin with དང་པོ་ནི but simply continue exposition (like "དང་པ་(ཚ་དམྱལ་)ནི། འཛམ་བུ་གླིང་འདི༷འི་འོག༷...") are NOT headings unless they clearly mark a new structural division.
The anchor must be copied exactly as it appears in the text, including all Tibetan characters, punctuation, and special marks (like ༷). Count carefully to extract exactly 40 characters starting from the first character of the heading.
