2026/11/02 13:25:10 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 9: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
IMPORTANT GUIDELINES FOR IDENTIFYING HEADINGS:
1. OUTLINE HEADINGS are marked by Tibetan ordinal markers followed by "ནི།" or similar particles: དང་པོ་...ནི། ("the first is"), གཉིས་པ་...ནི། ("the second is"), གསུམ་པ་...ནི། ("the third is"), བཞི་པ་...ནི། ("the fourth is"), etc. These mark structural divisions in the commentary.
2. INTRODUCTORY STATEMENTS that announce subdivisions (e.g., "ལ་གཉིས།" = "has two parts", "ལ་གསུམ།" = "has three parts", "ལ་བཞི།" = "has four parts", "ལ་དྲུག" = "has six parts") are ALSO outline headings. These statements introduce structural divisions by declaring how many sub-sections follow. They should be included as headings at the appropriate depth level. These introductory statements themselves ARE headings, not just announcements of headings to come.
3. NOT ALL ORDINALS ARE HEADINGS: Ordinal markers like དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་ that appear in the middle of sentences or discussions are NOT outline headings. Only those that introduce new structural sections are headings.
4. RHETORICAL QUESTIONS (ཞེ་ན།, "if you ask...") are NOT outline headings. These are common in Tibetan commentarial style to introduce objections or questions, but they do not mark structural divisions.
5. DEPTH DETERMINATION - CRITICAL: 
   a. When a passage begins with an ordinal followed by an introductory statement (e.g., བདུན་པ་...ལ་བཞི།), this introductory statement itself is a depth-1 heading that introduces the top-level structure for this passage.
   b. The subdivisions announced by such introductory statements (དང་པོ་...ནི།, གཉིས་པ་...ནི།, གསུམ་པ་...ནི།, བཞི་པ་...ནི།) are depth+1 (i.e., depth 2) relative to the introducing heading.
   c. Similarly, if the passage begins with an ordinal introducing multiple parts (e.g., བརྒྱད་པ་...གསུམ་བཤད་པ་ནི།), this is depth-1, and any subdivisions within are depth-2 or deeper.
   d. When a depth-2 section itself contains an introductory statement announcing further subdivisions (e.g., དང་པོ་...ལ་དྲུག), that introductory statement becomes a depth-2 heading, and its subdivisions are depth-3.
   e. Track the parent-child relationships carefully: each new subdivision increases depth by 1 from its immediate parent heading. The introductory statement and its announced subdivisions are at different depth levels.
6. The leading ༈ symbol typically marks major section headings and should be included in the anchor if present.
7. Parenthetical clarifications like (བསྟན་པ་གནས་པའི་ཚད་) or (དངོས་) that restate or label the topic are part of the heading structure and should be included in the anchor.
8. Copy the anchor text EXACTLY as it appears in the passage, including all tshegs (་), shads (།), special characters, and any formatting marks like ༷. The anchor must be the verbatim first 40 characters starting from the very first character of the heading in the passage.
9. THE INTRODUCTORY STATEMENT PATTERN: When you see a pattern like "[ordinal]པ་[topic]...ལ་[number]" (e.g., "བདུན་པ་དགྲ་བཅོམ་གྱི་དབྱེ་བ་ལ་བཞི།" meaning "the seventh, divisions of arhats, has four parts"), this ENTIRE statement is itself a heading at the current depth level. The subdivisions it announces (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, etc.) will be at depth+1.
