2026/11/02 13:44:59 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 20: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
IMPORTANT INSTRUCTIONS:
1. IDENTIFYING HEADINGS: Headings are marked by Tibetan ordinal numbers followed by structural phrases:
   - དང་པོ (first), གཉིས་པ (second), གསུམ་པ (third), བཞི་པ (fourth), ལྔ་པ (fifth), etc.
   - Followed by topic indicators like: ནི།, ལ་གསུམ།, ལ་བཞི།, ལ་ལྔ།, བཤད་པ་ནི།, དབྱེ་བ་ནི།, etc.
   - Examples: "གཉིས་པ་ཉེར་བསྡོགས་ནི།", "དང་པོ་(མཚམས་སྦྱར་)ནི།", "གསུམ་པ་ལམ་སུན་འབྱིན་པའི་སྒོ་ནས་དབྱེ་བ་ནི།"
2. DETERMINING DEPTH: 
   - Use the hierarchical context to determine depth, NOT the ordinal number itself
   - A heading's depth depends on which parent section it belongs to
   - When a passage begins with "གཉིས་པ" or "གསུམ་པ", this indicates it's a continuation of a larger outline - assign depth based on the structure WITHIN the passage
   - Headings introduced by phrases like "ལ་གསུམ།" or "ལ་བཞི།" announce sub-sections; the actual sub-sections follow with དང་པོ, གཉིས་པ, etc.
   - Example: "བཞི་པ་ཏིང་ངེ་འཛིན་གྱི་དབྱེ་བ་ལ་བཞི།" announces 4 sub-sections, then "དང་པོ་(ཁམས་ཀྱི་སྒོ་ནས་དབྱེ་བ་)" is the first sub-section with depth one level deeper
3. PARENTHETICAL CONTENT:
   - Content in parentheses like "(མཚམས་སྦྱར་)" or "(རྩ་བ་བཞི་)" is part of the heading and helps clarify the topic
   - Include this in the anchor but extract the key topic for the title
4. ANCHOR EXTRACTION:
   - Copy exactly the first 40 characters starting from the ordinal number
   - Include all Tibetan characters, punctuation (།), and spaces exactly as they appear
   - If the heading starts with ༈, include it in the anchor
5. TITLE CREATION:
   - Extract the main topic phrase, removing ordinal numbers and structural words
   - Keep the core meaning concise
   - Example: "གཉིས་པ་ལམ་གྱི་སྒོ་ནས་དབྱེ་བ་ནི།" → title: "ལམ་གྱི་སྒོ་ནས་དབྱེ་བ"
6. COMMON PATTERNS TO RECOGNIZE:
   - "དང་པོ་...ནི།" "གཉིས་པ་...ནི།" "གསུམ་པ་...ནི།" etc. = sequential headings
   - "ལ་གསུམ།" "ལ་བཞི།" "ལ་ལྔ།" = announces number of sub-sections to follow
   - "བཤད་པ་ནི།" "དབྱེ་བ་ནི།" "རང་བཞིན་ནི།" = topic descriptors
7. NOT HEADINGS:
   - Standalone topic lists (e.g., "དམིགས་ཡུལ་འཕགས་པའི་བདེན་པ། བདེན་པ་མངོན་པར་རྟོགས་པའི་རིམ་པ།") that appear after "ལ་བཞི།" are just previews, not actual headings
   - The actual headings come when these topics are introduced with ordinal numbers
