2026/11/02 13:47:19 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 22: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
IMPORTANT GUIDELINES FOR IDENTIFYING HEADINGS:
1. OUTLINE HEADINGS are marked by Tibetan ordinal markers followed by structural particles like "ནི།", "ལ་གཉིས།", "ལ་གསུམ།", or similar constructions that indicate divisions: དང་པོ་...ནི། ("the first is"), གཉིས་པ་...ནི། ("the second is"), གསུམ་པ་...ལ་གཉིས། ("the third has two parts"), བཞི་པ་...ནི། ("the fourth is"), etc. These mark structural divisions in the commentary.
2. COMPLETE HEADING IDENTIFICATION: A heading begins with the ordinal marker and continues through the structural particle and any parenthetical clarifications that immediately follow. For example, "དང་པོ་(ཚོར་བའི་དབང་པོ་ལྔ་བཤད་པ་)ནི།" is a complete heading where the parenthetical restatement is part of the heading structure. Similarly, "གསུམ་པ་བདེན་པ་མངོན་རྟོགས་ལ་གནས་པའི་གང་ཟག་ལ་གཉིས།" includes the topic description before "ལ་གཉིས།" as part of the heading.
3. STRUCTURAL DIVISION INDICATORS: Headings often explicitly indicate how many subdivisions follow, using phrases like "ལ་གཉིས།" (has two parts), "ལ་གསུམ།" (has three parts), "ལ་བཞི།" (has four parts). These division indicators are part of the heading and signal that subsequent ordinal headings will be sub-sections.
4. NOT ALL ORDINALS ARE HEADINGS: Ordinal markers like དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་ that appear in the middle of sentences or discussions without the structural particles are NOT outline headings. Only those that introduce new structural sections are headings. Context matters: ordinals in phrases like "བསམ་གཏན་གཉིས་པ་" (second meditation) or "ཚེ་གཉིས་" (two lifetimes) are descriptive, not structural.
5. RHETORICAL QUESTIONS (ཞེ་ན།, "if you ask..." or "གང་ཞེ་ན།" "what is it?") are NOT outline headings. These are common in Tibetan commentarial style to introduce objections or questions, but they do not mark structural divisions.
6. DEPTH DETERMINATION: 
   - Start by identifying the shallowest level headings in the passage (those with no parent sections). These are depth 1.
   - When a heading contains a division indicator ("ལ་གཉིས།", "ལ་གསུམ།", etc.), the subsequent ordinal headings (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་, etc.) that follow are its children at depth+1.
   - Sibling headings share the same parent and occur in ordinal sequence (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་, བཞི་པ་, etc.).
   - When a new sequence of ordinals (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་...) appears within a section already introduced by an ordinal heading, these new ordinals mark sub-sections and should be assigned depth+1 relative to their parent.
   - CRITICAL: Parenthetical clarifications like (དངོས་), (ངན་སོང་), (ཚ་དམྱལ་) within headings do NOT create new structural levels. They are labels for clarity. A heading "དང་པོ་(དངོས་)ལ་གཉིས།" announces two subdivisions, and those subdivisions (དང་པོ་..., གཉིས་པ་...) are at depth+1 relative to it, regardless of the parenthetical label.
   - Example structure:
     * དང་པོ་(དངོས་)ལ་གཉིས། (depth 1, announces two subdivisions)
       * དང་པོ་(ངན་སོང་)ལའང་གཉིས། (depth 2, first subdivision, announces two further subdivisions)
         * དང་པ་(ཚ་དམྱལ་)ནི། (NOT a heading - lacks proper ordinal marker དང་པོ་)
         * གཉིས་པ་གྲང་དམྱལ་ནི། (depth 2, SIBLING of དང་པོ་(ངན་སོང་), not child)
       * གཉིས་པ་མཐོ་རིས་ཀྱི་གནས་ནི། (depth 1, SIBLING of first དང་པོ་(དངོས་) heading - the pattern resets when the announced subdivisions are complete)
7. VARIANT ORDINAL FORMS: Be aware that དང་པོ་ can appear as དང་པ་ (without the final ོ). This is still a valid ordinal marker and should be recognized as such when followed by appropriate structural particles.
8. The leading ༈ symbol typically marks major section headings and should be included in the anchor if present.
9. Copy the anchor text EXACTLY as it appears in the passage, including all tshegs (་), shads (།), special characters, and subscript marks like ༷. The anchor should be the first 40 characters starting from the ordinal marker.
10. DEPTH CALCULATION REQUIRES TRACKING SUBDIVISION COMPLETION: After a heading announces subdivisions (ལ་གཉིས།, ལ་གསུམ།, etc.), you must track when those subdivisions are complete. Once all announced subdivisions have appeared in sequence (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, [གསུམ་པ་...]), the next ordinal heading that appears is typically at the same depth as the parent that announced those subdivisions, NOT a continuation of the subdivision sequence.
