2026/11/02 13:52:31 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 24: Proposed new text for extract.predict: You are given a passage of a Tibetan Buddhist commentary in which all formatting and line breaks have been removed. Find every outline heading (ས་བཅད, sa-bcad) in the passage, in reading order. For each heading return: depth (1 = the shallowest heading level present in this passage, 2 = a sub-section of a depth-1 heading, and so on), anchor (the first 40 characters of the heading copied verbatim from the passage, starting at its first character, including a leading ༈ if there is one) and title (a short label of the section). Return an empty list if the passage contains no headings.
Outline headings follow these patterns:
1. Headings typically begin with ordinal markers such as དང་པོ་ ("first"), གཉིས་པ་ ("second"), གསུམ་པ་ ("third"), བཞི་པ་ ("fourth"), and so on, often followed by a topic phrase and structural particles ལ་, ནི་, or division markers ལ་གཉིས།, ལ་གསུམ།, ལ་བཞི།, etc.
2. The structural phrase ལ་གཉིས། indicates the section is divided into two parts, ལ་གསུམ། into three parts, ལ་བཞི། into four parts, etc. These division markers signal that the following headings are subsections.
3. Subsections are introduced with ordinal markers that reference back to the parent division. For example, after "ལ་གཉིས།" (divided into two), you will typically find "དང་པོ་" (the first) and "གཉིས་པ་" (the second) as immediate children.
4. Sometimes the heading includes a parenthetical recap of the parent section title in parentheses after the ordinal, e.g., "དང་པོ་(ཆགས་བྲལ་སྔོན་སོང༌)ལ།" means "First (regarding those free from attachment proceeding)".
5. Depth is determined by the hierarchical nesting: top-level sections have depth 1, their immediate subsections have depth 2, subsections of those have depth 3, and so on. Track the parent-child relationships carefully by noting when a section announces subdivisions (ལ་གཉིས། etc.) and then look for the corresponding ordinal-marked subsections that follow.
6. The anchor must be copied character-for-character from the passage, including all Tibetan Unicode characters, spaces, and punctuation exactly as they appear. Do not normalize, transliterate, or modify the text in any way.
7. The title should be a concise descriptive label extracted from the heading phrase, typically the main topic portion without the ordinal and structural particles.
8. Headings may appear anywhere in the passage and are interspersed with explanatory content. Scan the entire passage systematically to identify all headings.
9. Pay special attention to phrases like "ཞུགས་གནས་གཉིས། དང་པོ་(ཞུགས་)" which means "[divided into] two: entering and abiding. First (entering)" — here the parent heading announces a two-part division, and the child heading "དང་པོ་" references the first part.
10. Not every sentence beginning with an ordinal is a heading. Context is important: headings are structural markers that organize the outline and are typically followed by explanatory content on that topic. Ordinal markers embedded in explanatory prose (e.g., when the text is discussing "the first dhyana" or "the second feeling") are NOT headings.
11. རྒྱུ་མཚན་དང༌། རྒྱུ་མཚན་ཅན་གྱི་གང་ཟག་གོ། is a pattern meaning "[divided into] two: the reason and the persons characterized by that reason" — the དང༌ is a connector indicating two topics, and the final གོ། or གོ punctuates the list of divisions.
12. When a heading announces subdivisions (e.g., ལ་གསུམ།), the depth of the following ordinal-marked subsections (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་) is one greater than the depth of the heading announcing the divisions.
13. Track the depth by maintaining a mental or explicit stack of the current outline level as you read through the passage. When a new subdivision is announced, push the context; when a subdivision sequence is complete (all announced parts have been covered), pop back to the parent level.
14. Always copy the anchor starting exactly at the first character of the heading as it appears in the passage, even if that means including preceding particles or markers that are part of the heading phrase.
15. Ensure every heading returned is actually present verbatim in the passage. Double-check that the anchor text can be found character-for-character in the input.
16. CRITICAL: A phrase like "བཞི་པ་མི་གཡོ་བར་གཞག་པའི་རྒྱུ་མཚན་ནི།" ("Fourth, the reason for establishing [it] as unmoving:") IS a structural heading when it introduces a new section. The ordinal (བཞི་པ་) + topic phrase (མི་གཡོ་བར་གཞག་པའི་རྒྱུ་མཚན) + particle (ནི།) pattern is the canonical heading form, even if it does not explicitly announce subdivisions.
17. CRITICAL DEPTH RULE FOR IMPLICIT SUBDIVISIONS: When you encounter a heading that does NOT explicitly announce subdivisions (e.g., "བཞི་པ་མི་གཡོ་བར་གཞག་པའི་རྒྱུ་མཚན་ནི།" without "ལ་གསུམ།" or similar), but is then FOLLOWED by consecutive ordinal-marked subsections starting from དང་པོ་ or any ordinal lower than the parent, these subsections ARE children of that heading, NOT siblings. The parent has an IMPLICIT subdivision structure. The children are at depth = (parent depth + 1). For example, if "བཞི་པ་...ནི།" is at depth 1, and is followed by "གཉིས་པ་...ནི།" and "གསུམ་པ་...ནི།" (consecutive ordinals elaborating sub-topics), then གཉིས་པ་ and གསུམ་པ་ are at depth 2, NOT depth 1.
18. CRITICAL: Distinguish between two cases: (a) Consecutive independent top-level headings (e.g., དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་ as siblings all at depth 1 when they are the primary structural divisions of THIS passage), and (b) A parent heading followed by subordinate headings that count as its subdivisions (even without explicit "ལ་གསུམ།" etc.). The key indicator for (b) is when a heading with an ordinal is followed by consecutive lower or restarting ordinals (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, etc.) that elaborate on sub-topics of that heading. In case (b), the subordinate headings are at depth = parent depth + 1.
19. When ordinal markers appear in explanatory prose (e.g., "བསམ་གཏན་གཉིས་པ་ན་ཡིད་བདེ། གསུམ་པ་ན་སེམས་བདེ།" meaning "in the second dhyana there is mental bliss, in the third there is mental bliss"), these are NOT headings. They are references within the explanation and lack the canonical heading structure (ordinal + topic + ལ་/ནི་).
20. If the passage begins with a heading like "གཉིས་པ་བརྩམ་པའི་བསྟན་བཅོས་ཀྱི་ལུས་དངོས་ལ་གཉིས།" ("Second, the actual body of the composed treatise [divided into] two:"), then the ordinals that follow (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་) are its children at depth 2, not siblings at depth 1.
21. CRITICAL DEPTH DETERMINATION ALGORITHM: To correctly assign depth, follow this procedure: (1) Identify all candidate headings (ordinal + topic + particle structure). (2) For each heading, check if it explicitly announces subdivisions (contains ལ་གཉིས།, ལ་གསུམ།, etc.). If yes, the immediately following ordinal-marked headings (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, etc.) are its children at depth = current depth + 1. (3) If a heading does NOT explicitly announce subdivisions but is followed by ordinals that restart or are lower than the parent ordinal, check the context: if these ordinals elaborate sub-topics of the parent heading, they are children at depth = parent depth + 1. (4) If ordinals are consecutive siblings at the same level (e.g., དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་ appearing as primary divisions of the passage), they all have the same depth. (5) When a sequence of sibling subdivisions is complete (all announced or contextually appropriate ordinals have appeared), the next heading with a higher or restarting ordinal may be a sibling of the parent or a new top-level section—determine based on ordinal value and context.
22. CRITICAL: In the third example provided, "གཉིས་པ་དེའི་ཁྱད་ཆོས་ལ་གཉིས།" is at depth 4, NOT depth 3. The structure is: (depth 1) གཉིས་པ་མཚུངས་ལྡན་མ་ཡིན་པའི་སྐྱེ་ཚུལ་ལ་གསུམ། > (depth 2) གཉིས་པ་རྒྱས་བཤད་ལ་བདུན། > (depth 3) དང་པོ་(ཐོབ་མ་ཐོབ་)ལ་གཉིས། > (depth 4) དང་པོ་(དངོས་)ནི། AND གཉིས་པ་དེའི་ཁྱད་ཆོས་ལ་གཉིས། The two depth-4 headings are SIBLINGS, both children of the depth-3 heading that announced "ལ་གཉིས།" (divided into two).
23. When a heading announces "ལ་གཉིས།" (divided into two), you should expect TWO immediate child headings marked དང་པོ་ and གཉིས་པ་ at the next depth level. Both of these children are at the SAME depth (one level deeper than the parent). Do not increment depth again for the second child; both དང་པོ་ and གཉིས་པ་ are siblings.
24. The pattern "[Ordinal]པ་...[topic]...ལ་[number]།" followed by "དང་པོ་...", "གཉིས་པ་...", etc. creates a clear parent-child structure where all the numbered subsections (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་, etc.) are siblings at depth = (parent depth + 1).
25. CRITICAL DEPTH RULE FROM EXAMPLE 1: In the first example, "བཞི་པ་མི་གཡོ་བར་གཞག་པའི་རྒྱུ་མཚན་ནི།" is at depth 1, and the headings "གཉིས་པ་འབྲས་བུ་སྐྱེ་བ་ན་ཚོར་བ་དུ་ཡོད་དཔྱད་པ་ནི།" and "གསུམ་པ་ཇི་ལྟར་འཐོབ་ཚུལ་ནི།" that follow are at depth 2 (children of བཞི་པ་). The feedback confirms this structure. Even though བཞི་པ་ does not explicitly say "ལ་གསུམ།" or similar, the presence of consecutive ordinal-marked subsections (གཉིས་པ་ and གསུམ་པ་) indicates an implicit subdivision structure where these are elaborating sub-topics of བཞི་པ་. The ordinals གཉིས་པ་ and གསུམ་པ་ here are NOT referring to "second" and "third" as top-level peers of བཞི་པ་, but rather as the second and third sub-topics within the བཞི་པ་ section (with an implicit first sub-topic likely embedded in the initial exposition after བཞི་པ་).
26. IMPLICIT SUBDIVISION IDENTIFICATION: When a heading (e.g., "བཞི་པ་...") is followed by ordinals that are LOWER or RESTART (e.g., གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་), treat these subsequent ordinals as children (depth + 1) if they introduce distinct sub-topics that elaborate the parent heading's subject. This is particularly clear when the parent ordinal is higher (e.g., བཞི་པ་ = fourth) and is followed by lower ordinals (e.g., གཉིས་པ་ = second, གསུམ་པ་ = third) that would not make sense as independent top-level siblings in sequence.
27. ORDINAL SEQUENCE LOGIC: In a normal top-level sequence, you expect དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་, བཞི་པ་, etc. in order. If you see བཞི་པ་ followed by གཉིས་པ་, གསུམ་པ་, this break in sequence is a strong indicator that གཉིས་པ་ and གསུམ་པ་ are NOT continuing the top-level sequence but are instead subdivisions of བཞི་པ་. The ordinal values restart or jump because they are enumerating parts within the བཞི་པ་ section.
28. CONTEXTUAL CLUES FOR DEPTH: The topic phrases provide context. If "བཞི་པ་མི་གཡོ་བར་གཞག་པའི་རྒྱུ་མཚན་ནི།" introduces the reason for establishing the fourth dhyana as unmoving, and "གཉིས་པ་འབྲས་བུ་སྐྱེ་བ་ན་ཚོར་བ་དུ་ཡོད་དཔྱད་པ་ནི།" discusses feelings in the resultant absorptions (a sub-topic related to the fourth dhyana's characteristics), this topical relationship supports treating གཉིས་པ་ as a child of བཞི་པ་.
29. ALWAYS VERIFY ANCHOR TEXT: Before finalizing your output, search for each anchor text in the original passage to confirm it exists exactly as you've extracted it. This is essential for accuracy.
30. TITLE EXTRACTION: The title should capture the essence of the heading's topic. Strip away ordinals (དང་པོ་, གཉིས་པ་, etc.), structural particles (ལ་, ནི་, etc.), and division markers (ལ་གཉིས།, etc.), leaving the core topic phrase. For example, from "བཞི་པ་མི་གཡོ་བར་གཞག་པའི་རྒྱུ་མཚན་ནི།" extract "མི་གཡོ་བར་གཞག་པའི་རྒྱུ་མཚན" as the title.
